# 6. Recap: useful exam practice

Attempt → mark → fix one gap → retry.

Start with one question.
Keep the first attempt.

---

# Find the exact task

Write year, level, section and question number.
Read the whole question and its command words.

For a full paper, read choices, marks, duration and materials.
Instructions differ across years.

---

# Explain means why or how

Weak: “The loop is wrong.”

Better: “The count never changes, so the condition
stays true and the loop cannot exit through that condition.”

Link the reason to the actual code or scenario.

---

# A programming checklist

Read the starter and save the required copy.
Add one requirement, run it, then add the next.
Predict normal, boundary and invalid results.
Check output order, spacing and saved filenames.

Prepare starter files before your practice timer.

---

# Mark with evidence

Use the matching scheme and its subpart allocations.
Underline evidence that earns credit.
Ask about uncertain alternative answers.

Record one precise gap, not “I am bad at this”.

---

# Turn a gap into a repair

Gap: only wrote the final sorted list.
Repair: trace a new list, recording every required swap.
Retry later: a fresh sorting question without notes.

A copied correction is not an independent retry.

---

# Longer sessions when ready

2025 Higher A/B practice budget: 5 + 32 + 45 + 8 = 90 min.
Its C budget: 5 + 40 + 10 + 5 = 60 min.
These are study suggestions within the printed totals.

Course mock: 100 marks, two 60-minute sessions.
Its format and marking are original teaching choices.

---

# Choose your next action

Pick one question, one repair or a full section.
Keep your work and check whether the skill improves.

[Open the past-paper library](https://tutors.dev/topic/lc-cs/topic-17-past-papers)
